}
---
{
 "id": "REL-RERUN-06",
 "criterion": "A tag push that GitHub refuses because the tagged commit's workflows differ from every branch tip fails with E180, naming the tag and target commit.",
 "is_error_case": true
}
---
{
 "id": "REL-RERUN-07",
 "criterion": "A local `callisto release` refuses with E320, before any effect, when the registry already holds a version it would release and the package's files differ from the commit that set that version, or no commit on the first-parent history sets it. The version is read from the package manifest or, for a Cargo package that inherits it, from the workspace manifest; files are compared by content, so a reverted change and a change made inside a merge commit count. The error names the version commit; checking it out and running `callisto release` again adopts the published version and tags that commit. A registry that cannot be read stops the release before any effect. A package whose manifest is not committed is not checked here; the clean-tree rule covers it. A package whose files match that commit, or whose version the registry does not yet hold, is unaffected.",
 "is_error_case": true
}
---
{
 "id": "REL-RERUN-08",
 "criterion": "`callisto release` and `callisto release --dry-run` print a warning on stderr for each package's last tag that exists in the repository but not on `origin` (one `git ls-remote` for all of them), saying its release may not have finished. The warning does not change the plan or the exit status, and nothing is printed when there is no `origin`, no tag, or the remote cannot be read."
}
---
{
 "id": "REL-RERUN-09",
 "criterion": "On a main push or manual dispatch without an explicit source, the repository's release workflow builds Callisto and takes the first release from `callisto release pending` as the source; with none pending, it examines the triggering commit. It rejects a selected source that is not an ancestor of the triggering main commit or is not the exact merge commit of exactly one GitHub-verified managed PR, rather than moving on to a newer release PR. Nothing is recorded on the PR; the committed decision and provider observations remain authoritative."
}
---
{
 "id": "REL-RERUN-10",
 "criterion": "After `execute` succeeds, including receipt upload, the workflow dispatches the oldest unfinished release other than the one just executed, by full merge SHA; when none remains, it refreshes the managed release PR for changesets now on main. A failed execution leaves the release unfinished and selected again. A failed dispatch fails the drain job and does not refresh the release PR. The drain job receives no registry secrets."
}
---
{
 "id": "REL-RERUN-11",
 "criterion": "`callisto release pending` lists, oldest first, the merged release commits whose release did not finish, from the last `--limit` commits (default 10) on HEAD's first-parent line that wrote the committed decision file (`--decision`, default `.callisto/release-decision.json`). A release is finished when every package in that commit's decision that has a publish target has a tag for its version, and every such package released to GitHub has a published (not draft) release; one that cannot be read counts as unfinished. The scan stops at the newest finished release, since releases are sequential. With `--from <sha>` only that release commit and newer ones count, and a SHA that is not a release commit on the branch is an error. The command lists the commit, its packages and versions, and what is missing, in text or in JSON under `pending`; it publishes nothing and exits 0 when there are none."
}
---
{
 "id": "REL-GH-07",
 "criterion": "In the release workflow, `CARGO_REGISTRY_TOKEN` reaches only the `execute` job's execute step. The workflow's default permissions are empty, and the plan and build jobs receive no registry credential."
}
---
{
 "id": "REL-PR-04",
 "criterion": "The release-PR action runs the version command with `--emit-decision <decision_path>` (default `.callisto/release-decision.json`). It commits the result with GitHub's `createCommitOnBranch` on `<release-branch>--staging` reset to the base commit, and refuses unless the remote tree equals the locally staged tree. It then moves the release branch ref through the REST API and never runs `git push`. Before creating the commit, before moving the ref, and before any label or PR write, it runs `callisto release-pr verify` against a fresh snapshot and stops when verification fails. After an update ref move is read back successfully, the final check supplies `--applied-head <new_sha>`. This accepts only a consistent update decision's selected PR changing to that exact head; any other snapshot change, wrong applied head, or create/noop use fails E148. The final check is retried for up to 27 seconds while the PR list still shows the old head, because the forge can lag a ref move; a head that never shows the applied commit still fails E148. Checks before the move and the create path retain strict original-snapshot equality."
}
---
{
 "id": "REL-PR-05",
 "criterion": "`callisto-action` with `mode: release` runs `callisto --format json release --receipt <tmp>` and sets `published` and `publishedPackages` from registry and platform publish operations whose outcome is `published`. A `mode` other than `version-pr` or `release` fails the step."
}
---
{
 "id": "REL-PR-06",
 "criterion": "With `source: auto`, `callisto-action` and `setup-callisto` default `version` input `latest` installs the callisto release the action's own invocation is pinned to (`github.action_ref` resolved against `orin-dx/callisto`'s `callisto@` tags), falling back to the newest published release only when that ref is unset or matches no tag. An explicit `version` input overrides this resolution, including inside a Callisto checkout. A consumer manifest mentioning callisto-cli never selects a local build. `source: local` ignores version pins and requires Git-tracked Callisto source at the checkout root plus Cargo metadata confirming the local callisto-cli workspace member, manifest path and callisto binary target. Local builds use explicit manifest and output-directory paths; invalid local source fails without falling back to a downloaded or registry installation."
}
---

